LRC(14) GENERAL 12-CORE CARRY-CONSERVATION (HYP-2661 generalized to ALL primitive cores)
kind-pasteur-2026-06-19.  EXACT rationals (fractions.Fraction), lonely measure at gap 1/14.

THR1 (mouth)        = 7/858      = 0.0081585...
THR2 (AP 2nd value) = 426/35035  = 0.0121592...

QUESTION (user angle): does HYP-2661 hold beyond AP-tail rows, for ARBITRARY primitive positive
12-cores C?  i.e.  meas(G_C) < 426/35035  ==>  {1,2,4,8} subset C   (carry[1]=15) ?

============================================================================================
RESULT 1 — EXHAUSTIVE over ALL primitive 12-subsets of [1,cap], cap=13..20:
  cap=13..18: the ONLY sub-THR2 core is drop-6 (1,2,3,4,5,7,8,9,10,11,12,13), meas 7/858, tower FULL.
  cap=20: exactly 2 sub-THR2 cores, BOTH tower-FULL:
     drop-6                              meas 7/858     =0.008159  tower {1,2,4,8} present
     {5:+2} exc (...,11,12,13,20)        meas 3859/420420=0.009179  tower {1,2,4,8} present
  => HYP-2661 generalized CONFIRMED exhaustively on every primitive 12-core in [1,20].
     (This is the FIRST test beyond the AP-tail family; codex verified only AP-tail 1-/2-tail rows.)

RESULT 2 — DIRECT MIN over TOWER-BROKEN cores (miss >=1 of {1,2,4,8}), primitive, [1,20]:
  113100 tower-broken primitive cores scanned.
  MINIMUM tower-broken meas = 911/57057 = 0.015966  at  C=(1,2,3,5,7,8,9,10,11,12,13,19)
  Since 911/57057 = 0.015966 >= 426/35035 = 0.012159, NO tower-broken core beats THR2.
  => contrapositive of HYP-2661: tower-broken ==> meas >= THR2.  CONFIRMED with margin ~0.0038.

RESULT 3 — PER-BIT cost of breaking ONE tower element (keep the other three), [1,20]:
  break ONLY 1 (keep 2,4,8): min 1333/30940 = 0.043083
  break ONLY 2 (keep 1,4,8): min   27/1547  = 0.017453
  break ONLY 4 (keep 1,2,8): min  911/57057 = 0.015966   <- CHEAPEST defect (binding)
  break ONLY 8 (keep 1,2,4): min 6163/336336= 0.018324
  Ordering 4 < 8 < 2 < 1: breaking the middle bit 4 costs the least; breaking 1 (=d=1, the
  coarsest constraint) costs the most.  All four stay above THR2.  (Sharper than codex's earlier
  tower-deletion min for bit 4 = 335/23023=0.01455: that was AP-tail restricted; the true
  free-core min is 911/57057, still >THR2.)

============================================================================================
WHY THE TOWER / WHY 4 LEVELS / APEX-7 (third user question):
  theta = 1/N with N=14 = 2*7.  The dyadic-1 tower = { 2^a : 2^a < N } = {1,2,4,8} since 8<14<16.
  DEPTH = #{powers of 2 below N} = floor(log2(N-1))+1 = floor(log2 13)+1 = 4.
  The largest tower bit 8 = 2^3 is the largest power of 2 below 14; 2^4=16 > 14 stops the tower.
  So "4 levels" is exactly floor(log2 N).  The apex prime 7 enters as the OTHER factor of N=2*7:
  HYP-2656's CRT split 1/14=(1/2)(1/7) makes the odd skeleton {1,3,..,13} 7-adic (rigid, constant
  measure) and the EVEN/doubling content {2,4,8,...} the 2-adic tower that the law constrains.

  N-GENERAL CAVEAT (important, NEW): the tower law is NOT a universal LRC(2p) phenomenon.
  Tested the analogous one-hole mouths at N=6,10,14:
     N=6  mouth = drop-2  -> tower {1,2,4} BROKEN (missing 2);  law FALSE at N=6.
     N=10 mouth = drop-4  -> tower {1,2,4,8} BROKEN (missing 4); law FALSE at N=10.
     N=14 mouth = drop-6  -> tower {1,2,4,8} FULL;  law TRUE at N=14.
  The tower survives at N=14 *because the mouth hole is 6* (= 2*3, an even non-power-of-2), so it
  does not touch {1,2,4,8}.  At N=6,10 the mouth hole IS a power of 2 (2, resp. 4), breaking the
  tower.  CONCLUSION: the dyadic-1 tower is the mouth-owner for the SPECIFIC N=14 core geometry,
  not a universal LRC mouth-owner.  This is a feature of where the apex hole lands relative to the
  powers of two below N.
